<Legal
	title="Privacy policy"
	description="What the Leuria app sends, where, and what it keeps on your computer."
	updated="1 October 2026"
>
	<p>
		Leuria runs on your computer. There is no Leuria server and no Leuria account, and the app sends no usage data or analytics. This page
		lists everything the app itself connects to, and why.
	</p>

	<h2>What Leuria keeps, on your computer only</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>The websites you connected, and the AI and model you chose for each.</li>
		<li>Your sign-in to the AI apps you set up, and the keys of AI services you add.</li>
		<li>The AI apps it downloaded for you.</li>
	</ul>
	<p>It keeps nothing of your conversations. All of this is in one folder, <code>.leuria</code> in your home folder.</p>

	<h2>What Leuria connects to</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Updates.</strong> About every six hours, Leuria asks
			<a href="https://github.com/leur-ia/leuria/releases">GitHub</a> whether a newer version exists, and downloads it if so. The request
			carries nothing about you beyond what any download does, such as your IP address.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>The list of AI apps.</strong> To show which AI apps you can use, Leuria reads a
			<a href="https://cdn.agentclientprotocol.com/registry/v1/latest/registry.json">public list of AI apps</a>, kept by their publishers. When you pick one, Leuria downloads it from where
			its publisher offers it (for example npm or GitHub).
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>The AI you choose.</strong> Your questions go to that AI, under your own account, as when you use it directly. Its own privacy
			policy applies: for example <a href="https://openai.com/policies/privacy-policy/">OpenAI</a>,
			<a href="https://www.anthropic.com/legal/privacy">Anthropic</a> or <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google</a>. With a model
			on your computer, your questions never leave it.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>The websites you connect.</strong> A website gets answers from your AI only after you approve it in Leuria's window, and only
			while it stays connected. If it offers instructions for your AI (skills), Leuria downloads them from the address the site gives.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Leuria sends nothing else to any other system, unless you ask it to. Its code is open source, so you can check this in the
		<a href="https://github.com/leur-ia/leuria">repository</a>.
	</p>

	<h2 id="this-website">This website</h2>
	<p>
		This section covers leuria.eu, the developer site leuria.dev and the demos on demo.leuria.dev. Each asks before counting your visit. If you say yes, it loads Google Analytics, which sets cookies and sends Google the pages
		you view, roughly where you are and what device you use, so we can see which pages help. Nothing is used for advertising. If you say
		no, Google is never contacted and no cookie is set. Your answer is kept in your browser, for each site, and you can change it any time with
		<button type="button" class="inline-link" data-consent-open>Visit counting</button> at the bottom of the page. See
		<a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google's privacy policy</a>.
	</p>
	<p>
		The sites are hosted on GitHub Pages, which may log visits under
		<a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub's privacy statement</a>.
	</p>

	<h2>Questions</h2>
	<p>Ask on <a href="https://github.com/leur-ia/leuria/issues">GitHub</a>.</p>
</Legal>

